00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.347
So the first question,
which had a lot of upvotes--

00:01:45.347 --> 00:01:47.680
meaning many people wanted
the answer to this question--

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.480
came from Jackson Lipfert.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.450
And the question was about
so-called ultradian rhythms.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.730
For those of you that are
not familiar with ultradian

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.160
rhythms-- ultradian
rhythms are any rhythms

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.710
that are shorter than 24 hours.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.810
And typically when
people ask about

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.730
or talk about
ultradian rhythms, they

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.830
are referring to
90-minute rhythms.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.810
I've talked about these
on the podcast before.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.500
And Jackson's question was--
how do you use ultradian rhythms

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.180
in your daily work?

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.640
There's more to the
question, but first off, I

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.410
do use ultradian
rhythms-- that is,

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:21.370
I leverage the fact that
these do exist in all of us

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.270
as a way to engage in
focused bouts of mental work

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.180
once or twice, or sometimes
three times per day.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.540
However, I use them
in a way that's

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.150
grounded in the research on
ultradian rhythms for learning

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.952
and memory in a
way that might not

00:02:35.952 --> 00:02:37.660
be obvious just from
their name that they

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.980
are 90-minute rhythms.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.520
So I'll get into the details
of how to use ultradian rhythms

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.528
to best capture
neuroplasticity--

00:02:44.528 --> 00:02:46.570
that is the brain's ability
to change in response

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.470
to experience-- and in a way
that should allow you to get

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:54.700
one or two, or maybe even three
focused bouts of learning per

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.830
day, which can greatly
accelerate learning

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.220
of cognitive material,
languages, mathematics,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.060
history--

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.710
for sake of school
or work, or maybe

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.020
just a hobby, or a personal
interest of some sort--

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.900
and for skill learning in
the physical domain as well.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.430
Jackson then went on to ask--

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.930
you've mentioned
before that you try

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.572
to include at least one
90-minute focus block per day

00:03:13.572 --> 00:03:15.280
as part of your work
and overall mission.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.420
And indeed, that is true.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.420
I tried to get at least one of
these focused ultradian rhythm

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.980
blocks per day.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.190
That is a period
of about 90 minutes

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.710
where I'm focused on learning
something or doing something

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.170
that's cognitively hard,
although typically I

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.510
aim for two of these
sessions per day.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.210
He then goes on to ask-- what
is the maximum number of blocks

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.160
you can perform sustainably?

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.400
The answer to that
is probably four.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.460
And I say probably because
some people have schedules,

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:46.450
lifestyles, in which
four 90-minute blocks

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.930
of focused learning
is possible per day,

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.250
but that's highly unusual.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.670
For most people, it's going to
be one or two, maybe three--

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.130
four, I would place in the
really extraordinary end

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.380
of things, maybe if
you're cramming for exams

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:02.090
or you've managed to go on a
writing retreat or a learning

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.000
and retreat of some sort where
you can devote essentially,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.333
all of your non-sleeping,
non-eating time to learning.

00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:11.750
But most people simply can't
organize their life that way.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.330
So the short answer is--

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.690
for me, it's one or two
per day is the target

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.060
and three would be the maximum.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.220
You then went on to ask--
do you take vacations

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.550
or extended breaks from these
ultradian rhythm sessions?

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.130
And the short answer is no.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.793
Typically, I try and do this
every day-- and yes, even

00:04:29.793 --> 00:04:30.460
on the weekends.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.770
But on the weekend,
the ultradian rhythm

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.360
focused learning
bout might just be

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.400
reading a book for
about 90 minutes

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.450
or so, which might not be as
cognitively difficult as it

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.970
is for other sorts of work
that I perform during the week.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:44.890
I occasionally
miss a day entirely

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.650
for whatever reason--
travel, obligations

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.570
related to family, et cetera.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.940
But in general, I try
and do this every day.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.360
I do think that the
circuits for focus are,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.120
I guess the non-biological way
to put it would be kept warm.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.360
But essentially, that accessing
the circuits for focus

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.182
is made easier by
accessing them regularly

00:05:04.182 --> 00:05:05.890
and that's because
the circuits for focus

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:09.680
are indeed themselves
amenable to neuroplasticity.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.170
In other words, the more
you force yourself to focus,

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.015
the easier focusing gets.

00:05:14.015 --> 00:05:15.890
I'll now answer the last
part of the question

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.890
and then I will go through
and emphasize some tools

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:22.610
that anyone can use in order
to leverage ultradian rhythms

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.520
toward learning bouts--
either cognitive learning

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.978
or physical skill learning,
or a combination of the two.

00:05:28.978 --> 00:05:30.770
The last part of the
question Jackson asked

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.410
was-- if you knew you needed
to drastically increase

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.110
the amount of focus you do
daily, how would you schedule

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.210
that focus and recover from it?

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:41.270
That's an excellent
aspect to this question.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.190
And I will now give
you the details

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.130
of how I would use and
schedule ultradian rhythms.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.150
I'll offer you a tool--

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.070
I've never talked about this
tool in the Huberman Lab

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:49.730
podcast.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.300
And I will dispel a common myth
about ultradian rhythms that

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.420
points to a, believe it or
not, an easier way to leverage

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.180
them for maximum benefit.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:58.680
OK.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.710
So as I mentioned before,
ultradian rhythms are

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.770
these 90-minute cycles that
we go through from the time

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.200
that we are born
until the time we die.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.990
Indeed, even during
sleep, we are

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.330
experiencing and
more or less governed

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.250
by these ultradian rhythms.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:16.023
This question and this answer
is not so much about sleep.

00:06:16.023 --> 00:06:18.440
But just know that when you
go to sleep at night until you

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.840
wake up in the morning,
every 90 minutes or so,

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.650
your patterns of sleep-- that is
the percentage or ratio rather

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.410
of slow wave sleep to light
sleep to rapid eye movement

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:32.905
sleep changes in a way such
that each 90-minute cycle gates

00:06:32.905 --> 00:06:33.530
the next cycle.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.410
It flips on switch for
the next 90-minute cycle

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.360
then that 90-minute
cycle ends, flips

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.500
on switch for the next one,
and so on and so forth.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.500
I mentioned all that
because during the daytime,

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.420
the same thing is true.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.720
But most people don't know
when the 90-minute all trading

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:48.793
cycles begin.

00:06:48.793 --> 00:06:50.210
Because if you
think about it, you

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.810
could wake up on the
basis of an alarm clock

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.720
or noise in the room, or
simply because you naturally

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.210
wake up in the middle of an
ultradian 90-minute cycle.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.280
So does that mean, for instance,
that if you wake up 60 minutes

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.430
into an ultradian cycle
that the next 30 minutes

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:08.600
of your waking--

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.310
right, because that
60 minutes needs

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:12.170
to continue to 90 to
complete an ultradian

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:15.650
cycle-- that the next
30 minutes after waking

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.470
are related to the
ultradian cycle

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.500
that you were still in
during sleep, or does

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.810
it start a new ultradian cycle.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.310
And the answer is the former.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:28.250
That ultradian cycle
continues even if you wake up

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:29.520
in the middle of it.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.133
And so a lot of people who want
to leverage ultradian cycles

00:07:32.133 --> 00:07:34.550
for learning will say, "Well,
how do I know when to start?

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.440
When does it start,
when I hit my stopwatch?

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.750
Can I just set a clock
and work for 90 minutes?"

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.620
And the short answer is no.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.280
And that might seem
unfortunate, but the good news

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.140
is that you can figure out when
your first proper ultradian

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.950
cycle of the day begins simply
by asking yourself when are

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.530
you most alert after waking.

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:57.230
That is if you were, say
to wake up at 7:00 AM

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.710
and let's say that's the end of
an ultradian cycle or perhaps

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:02.580
you're in the middle
of an ultradian cycle--

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.070
doesn't matter.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.350
What you need to watch
for or pay attention to

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.240
for a day or so
is when you start

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.380
to experience your greatest
state of mental alertness

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:13.200
in the morning.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.680
And here, we can discard with
all the issues and variables

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.130
around caffeine or no caffeine,
hydrating or no hydrating.

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.620
Exercise is one variable that
we'll consider in a moment.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.560
But here's the deal--

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.560
these all trading in cycles
are actually triggered

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.360
by fluctuations in the so-called
glucocorticoid system--

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.120
the system that regulates
cortisol release.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.480
And as some of you have
probably heard me say before--

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:41.600
cortisol, even though it's often
discussed as a terrible thing,

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.020
it's chronic stress, cortisol,
cortisol, et cetera-- cortisol

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.450
is essential for
health and every day we

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:51.020
get a rise in cortisol in the
morning that is associated

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:54.020
with enhanced immune function,
enhanced alertness, enhanced

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:56.370
ability to focus,
so on and so forth.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.033
In fact, the protocol
that I'm always

00:08:58.033 --> 00:08:59.450
beating the drum
about that people

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:01.100
should get sunlight
in their eyes

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:03.830
as close to waking as possible--
that actually enhances

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:06.710
or increases the peak
level of cortisol that's

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.540
experienced early in the day.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.340
And that sets in motion a number
of these ultradian cycles.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.280
So for instance, if
you wake up at 7:00 AM

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.860
and you find that for the
first hour after waking,

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.990
you tend to be a
little bit groggy,

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.180
or you happen to be
groggy on a given day,

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.060
but then you notice that your
attention and alertness starts

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:27.170
to peak somewhere around
9:30 AM or 10:00 AM,

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:31.040
you can be pretty sure
that, that first ultradian

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.490
cycle for learning is going
to be optimal to start

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.650
at about 9:30 or 10:00 AM.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:40.340
How can I say about if it's
indeed a 90-minute cycle?

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:43.853
Well, this is really where
the underlying neurobiology

00:09:43.853 --> 00:09:45.770
in these ultradian cycles
converge to give you

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:47.000
a specific protocol.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.400
The changes in cortisol that
occur throughout the day

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:49.940
involve--

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:51.690
yes, a big peak early
in the day if you're

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.140
getting your sunlight and
caffeine and maybe even some

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:55.730
exercise early in the day.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.040
But typically, that
peak comes early.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:01.130
And then across the day the
baseline jitters a little bit,

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.230
it comes down, but it
bounces around a little bit.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:05.090
It's not a flat line,
if we were to measure

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:07.380
your glucocorticoid levels.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:10.160
Each one of those little
bumps corresponds to a shift

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:12.210
in these ultradian cycles.

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:16.310
So if you find that you
are most alert at 9:30

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.320
or starting to
become alert at 9:30,

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.050
and then typically you have a
peak of focus and concentration

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.070
around 10:00 AM, that is
really valuable to know.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:28.100
Because the way that
the molecules that

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.970
control neuroplasticity--
that is the changes in neurons

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.680
and other cell types
in the brain that

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.440
allow your nervous system
to learn and literally

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.900
for new connections to
form between neurons, which

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:40.670
is basically the
basis of learning--

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:44.570
those fluctuate according
to these ultradian cycles.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:45.690
What does this mean?

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:48.890
This means if your peak
in alertness and focus

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:50.000
and energy--

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.220
could even be experienced
as physical energy occurs

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:56.060
at about 9:30 AM, I would start
your first ultradian cycle

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:57.920
for learning somewhere
around there.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.990
Certainly 9:30 AM
would be ideal,

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:01.860
but 10:00 AM would
be fine as well.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.580
And then you have
about one hour to get

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.560
the maximum amount
of learning in even

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.100
within that alternating cycle.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.620
This is where there's a
lot of confusion out there,

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:12.000
people think, oh, ultradian
cycles are 90 minutes,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.500
therefore, we should be
in our peak level of focus

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:16.730
throughout that 90 minutes.

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.280
In reality, most people
take about 10 or 15 minutes

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.100
to break into a really
deep trench of focus

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.620
and then periodically
throughout the next hour,

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.810
they'll pop out
of that focus, now

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:28.430
have to deliberately refocus.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:30.380
This is why, if
possible, you want

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:31.967
to turn off Wi-Fi
connections and put

00:11:31.967 --> 00:11:33.800
your phone in the other
room or turn it off.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:34.100
If.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:35.643
You do need your
phone or Wi-Fi, just

00:11:35.643 --> 00:11:37.310
be aware of how
distracting those things

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:40.370
can be to getting into
a deep trench of focus.

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:42.500
But the point is this--

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.690
these 90-minute cycles
occur periodically

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.340
throughout the day,
but there is going

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:47.988
to be one period
early in the day--

00:11:47.988 --> 00:11:50.030
and here, I'm referring
to the spirit of starting

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:52.550
at about 9:30 or 10:00
AM-- and then likely

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:56.300
another one in the mid to late
afternoon that are going to be

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:59.150
ideal for focused learning.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:02.810
And that focus learning
bout should ideally

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.760
have you set your clock--

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.480
a stopwatch or something--
to measure 90 minutes, but do

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.320
assume that there's going to
be some jitter at the front end

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.070
where you're not going
to be able to focus

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:14.237
as deeply as you would like.

00:12:14.237 --> 00:12:16.738
Then you'll get about
an hour of deep focus

00:12:16.738 --> 00:12:18.530
and then you really
start to transition out

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:20.580
of these ultradian cycles.

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:23.300
How do when the afternoon
ultradian cycle occurs?

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.550
Well, just as in the
morning, it occurs

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:28.610
because there's a brief,
but significant increase

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:32.520
in the glucocorticoid system
in the mid to late afternoon.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.580
I wish I could tell you
it's going to be 2 PM

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:38.330
or it's going to be 3:00
PM-- that's really going

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:39.598
to depend on the individual.

00:12:39.598 --> 00:12:41.390
When you ingest caffeine,
some of the other

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:42.710
demands of your day.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:45.410
But you can learn
to recognize when

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.200
these two periods for
optimized learning

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.630
will occur and here
are the key principles.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.790
Watch for a day or two--
meaning pay attention

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.220
to when you have your peak
levels of physical and mental

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:58.250
energy in the morning-- that
is between waking and noon,

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:02.248
and then again between noon
and about 6:00 or 7:00 PM.

00:13:02.248 --> 00:13:04.790
Although I'm sure that there
are some late shifted folks that

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:07.010
will experience their peak in
focus somewhere around 6:00

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:08.802
or 7:00 PM, especially
if they're waking up

00:13:08.802 --> 00:13:12.230
around 10:00 or 11:00 AM, as I
know some people out there are.

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:14.690
Once you know where
those peaks in focus

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:18.500
occur on your schedule,
set a stopwatch

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:21.530
for one ultradian cycle in
the early part of the day.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:23.330
In this example,
I was saying 9:30,

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:26.000
but if you can't hop on
it until 10, that's fine.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.880
Set it for 90 minutes,
consider that block wholly--

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:30.860
meaning rule out all
other distractions.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.080
But assume that within
that 90-minute block,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.540
you are only going to be
able to focus intensely

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:37.430
for about one hour.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:40.613
And just know that the molecules
that control neuroplasticity--

00:13:40.613 --> 00:13:41.780
and these things have names.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:44.750
And yes, Brain Derived
Neurotrophic Factor or BDNF

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:47.700
is the most famous of those,
but there are others as well.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:49.940
In fact, the very
receptors that control

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:52.410
synaptic strength, the
connections between neurons,

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.980
some of the neurotransmitters
and modulators involved

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.740
in synaptic plasticity,
they undergo regulation

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.760
by these ultradian
changes in glucocorticoid.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.510
And then try and capture
a second ultradian

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:07.520
learning block in the afternoon.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:09.740
Again, just knowing that
the first 10 or 15 minutes,

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:11.407
consider it mental
warm up, and then you

00:14:11.407 --> 00:14:14.180
get about an hour-- it's not
exactly 60 minutes, but about

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.525
an hour to maximize learning.

00:14:15.525 --> 00:14:17.150
So if you're trying
to learn something,

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.260
really capture it during
that phase as well.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.410
Now, is there a
third opportunity

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:22.910
or a fourth opportunity?

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:25.460
This relates to Jackson's
question directly.

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:27.650
And the short answer
is not really,

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:30.170
unless you're somebody who
requires very little sleep.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.420
Within the 12 or
16 hours that one

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.670
tends to be awake during
the day or 18 hours

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:37.130
that one tends to be awake,
there are really only two

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:39.710
of these major peaks in the
glucocorticoid system that

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.720
trigger the onset of
the circadian cycles.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:45.530
Again, there's a ramping
up and a ramping down

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:47.280
of glucocorticoids
throughout the day.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:48.680
But the real key
here is to learn

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.110
when you tend to be
most focused based

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.180
on your regular sleep,
wake cycle, caffeine

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:54.710
intake, exercise, et cetera.

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:56.990
And again, that's going to
vary from person to person.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.660
And you really only have two
opportunities or two ultradian

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:03.410
cycles to capture in order
to get the maximum focus

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.930
challenging work done a.k.a.

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:06.660
learning.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.300
So for somebody
that wants to learn

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:11.030
an immense amount of material
or who has the opportunity

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:14.990
to capture another
ultradian cycle,

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:18.810
the other time where that tends
to occur is also early days.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:21.020
So some people by
waking up early

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:23.840
and using stimulants like
caffeine and hydration

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:25.940
or some brief
high-intensity exercise,

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:29.210
can trigger that cortisol
pulse to shift a little bit

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.550
earlier so that they can
capture a morning work

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.500
block that occurs
somewhere, let's

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.480
say between 6:00 and 7:30 AM.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.610
So let's think about
our typical person--

00:15:37.610 --> 00:15:41.030
at least in my example that's
waking up around 7:00 AM.

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:44.090
And then I said, has their first
ultradian work cycle really

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:47.910
flip on because that bump in
cortisol around 9:30 or 10:00

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:48.410
AM.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:53.390
If that person were say to set
their alarm clock for 5:30 AM,

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:57.980
then get up, get some artificial
light-- if the sun isn't out,

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.810
turn on bright or
artificial lights,

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:02.510
or if the sun happens to
be up that time of year,

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:04.010
get some sunlight in your eyes.

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:07.180
But irrespective
of sunlight, were

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:09.100
to get a little bit of
brief high intensity

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:12.190
exercise maybe 10 or 15 minutes
of skipping rope or even

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:15.460
just Jumping Jacks or go
out for a brief jog, what

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:18.680
happens then is the cortisol
pulse starts to shift earlier.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:20.602
And so the next day
and the following day

00:16:20.602 --> 00:16:22.810
and so on and so forth--
provided they're still doing

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:25.720
that exercise first thing and
ideally getting some light

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:26.800
in their eyes as well--

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:28.240
well then they
have an opportunity

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:30.340
to capture an increase
in cortisol that

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:34.212
is now shifted from about
7:00 AM to about 8:30 AM.

00:16:34.212 --> 00:16:35.920
So they can capture
an hour of work there

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:40.030
and then they will also
still be within that rising

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:42.775
phase of cortisol
in the 9:30 to 10:00

00:16:42.775 --> 00:16:47.200
AM block that lasts
until about 11:30 or so.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:48.190
They might have lunch.

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:50.230
Perhaps after lunch, they
do a Non-Sleep Deep Rest--

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:52.790
maybe they, don't maybe you're
a napper or maybe you're not--

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:53.990
doesn't really matter.

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.440
And then in the afternoon--

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:58.360
and I would suspect
it would now be

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:01.300
in the earlier afternoon
sometime around 2:00 or 2:30

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:02.560
would be typical.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:06.670
Although again, that exact time
will vary person to person.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:09.760
Then they would want to schedule
another 90-minute work block.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.349
So that's how you
can capture three.

00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:13.180
Now you can start
to see also why

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:15.430
capturing four
ultradian work blocks

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:16.880
would be exceedingly rare.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:19.583
It's just not typical
that people are awake

00:17:19.583 --> 00:17:22.000
for that much of the day, you
have to sleep at some point.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:23.740
And I should mention that if
you're going to force yourself

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.540
to wake up earlier on
a consistent basis,

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.722
you probably should be
trying to get to sleep

00:17:27.722 --> 00:17:28.930
a little bit earlier as well.

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.347
Because it's not
just the quality,

00:17:30.347 --> 00:17:32.855
but the duration of
quality sleep that really

00:17:32.855 --> 00:17:33.730
matters for learning.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.430
And I should also
remind everybody

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:39.070
that the actual
rewiring of neurons

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:41.320
does not occur during
any focused work block,

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.720
it actually occurs during
deep sleep the following night

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.100
and the following night, and
during Non-Sleep Deep Rest.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.910
This is why Non-Sleep
Deep Rest can accelerate

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:50.960
learning because it's
in states of rest

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.900
that the actual
connections between neurons

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:55.060
strengthen or weaken
or new neurons

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:58.190
are added in a way that allows
for what we call learning.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:17:58.690
OK.

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:02.980
So one or two all trading work
blocks per day is typical,

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.410
three would be
really exceptional,

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.780
and four would be extraordinary.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:12.280
Look for them-- meaning look to
see when you are feeling most

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.160
focused and alert
typically in the period

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.620
before waking and
noon and typically

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:20.140
in the period between
noon and bedtime,

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:23.920
given your standard intake
of caffeine and exercise

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:26.200
and other life events.

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:29.140
Please also remember
that even though it's

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.570
an ultradian
90-minute work block,

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:34.300
the neuroplasticity is
going to be best triggered

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:37.240
within a 60-minute
portion of that.

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.790
And there's no way to
know exactly when that 60

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:42.185
minutes begins and
ends until you actually

00:18:42.185 --> 00:18:43.060
begin the work block.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:45.177
So this is really
designed to be empirical,

00:18:45.177 --> 00:18:46.510
you need to actually go do this.

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:47.830
What you'll notice
again is that it's

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:49.247
hard to focus at
first then you'll

00:18:49.247 --> 00:18:50.440
drop into a state of focus.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.540
You may get distracted,
that's perfectly normal.

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:54.118
You refocus, get
back into triggering

00:18:54.118 --> 00:18:55.910
learning-- that's really
what you're doing,

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:58.210
you're triggering learning--
and then there'll be some taper

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.335
and then you'll be out of
the ultradian work block.

00:19:00.335 --> 00:19:03.280
Now, it's also a
key to understand

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:05.740
that myself and other
people should not

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.640
expect that they're only working
during these 90-minute work

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:09.140
blocks.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.660
It's just that a lot of the
sorts of demands of our day,

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:17.070
including cooking and shopping
for groceries, and email,

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.570
and text messaging, and social
media-- a lot of those things

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:21.670
don't require intense
focus of the sort

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:24.253
that I believe Jackson is
asking about maximizing,

00:19:24.253 --> 00:19:26.920
and that I'm referring to when I
talk about these ultradian work

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:27.520
blocks.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.223
And then as a final
point, I've been

00:19:29.223 --> 00:19:31.390
talking about these ultradian
work blocks and focus,

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:34.750
et cetera, in a context
that brings to mind ideas

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:36.160
about cognitive work.

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:38.860
So learning a language,
learning math, writing,

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.740
or creating, doing something
related to music, et cetera.

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:44.530
But these 90-minute
ultradian work blocks

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:48.700
also directly relate to
physical skill learning as well

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:50.570
and to physical
exercise as well.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:53.560
So if you are somebody who's
really interested in improving

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.390
your fitness and
your fitness requires

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:57.100
a lot of focused attention--

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:00.290
so for instance, when I go
out for a long run on Sundays,

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.290
which is part of
my fitness routine,

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:04.047
I deliberately not
thinking about much,

00:20:04.047 --> 00:20:05.380
I'm just trying to cruise along.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:06.922
I might focus a
little bit on my pace

00:20:06.922 --> 00:20:10.300
in stride, maybe an audiobook
I'm listening to or a podcast.

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:12.820
But typically, I'm
just cruising along,

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:14.980
it's low cognitive demand work.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:16.780
These ultradian work
blocks can really

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:20.620
be maximized for
pure cognitive work--

00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:23.890
book type work, et cetera,
music, et cetera, or they

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:26.810
can also be leveraged
toward skill learning.

00:20:26.810 --> 00:20:29.050
So if you're trying
to learn how to dance

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:31.240
or how to perform a
particular athletic move

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:34.090
or you're trying to get better
at some skill that requires

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:39.580
a lot of focus and alignment of
muscular movement and cognitive

00:20:39.580 --> 00:20:41.920
demands, et cetera,
well then these

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:44.590
are also going to be ideal
for triggering neuroplasticity

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:49.450
to get better in the motor skill
based domain as it's called,

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:50.140
et cetera.

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:52.690
If you'd like to learn
more about ultradian

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.020
shifts in neuroplasticity
and ultradian work bouts,

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:58.900
I will certainly do more on this
in the standard Huberman Lab

00:20:58.900 --> 00:20:59.710
podcast.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:01.918
But the key words
to look up if you

00:21:01.918 --> 00:21:03.460
want to explore this
further online--

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:05.740
it's not something that
a lot of people about,

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:08.250
it's called iterative
metaplasticity.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.530
It's a vast literature
and one that I'd

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:12.900
be happy to teach you in a
standard podcast episode.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:16.500
But in the interest of getting
to more questions from you

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:18.930
all, hopefully the
answer I've given

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:23.280
you now has been complete
enough, yet clear enough,

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:26.280
and yet succinct enough
that you can start

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.590
to leverage these
really powerful aspects

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:32.460
of iterative metaplasticity and
ultradian rhythms for learning.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:35.700
And I'd just like to point
out that these opportunities

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:39.870
for focused learning that occur
in these 90-minute ultradian

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.690
cycles are really
terrific opportunities.

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:45.780
They are offered to you
at least twice every day

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:48.660
and you can really learn
to detect when they occur

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:50.590
and when they're
likely to occur.

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:54.370
You can certainly learn at other
times in the 24-hour cycle.

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:57.420
But for anyone who's tried to
stay up late at night cramming

00:21:57.420 --> 00:21:59.340
for an exam or
for somebody who's

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:02.460
tried to learn during
the sleepiest time

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:07.110
of their afternoon, we can be
very familiar with the fact

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:10.320
that there are times of day in
which we are best at learning.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:12.390
And as I've just
described, there

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:14.760
are ways to capture
those moments

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:16.530
and they are valuable moments.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.187
So even though it's just
about three hours per day

00:22:19.187 --> 00:22:21.270
or really only two hours
per day because of the 60

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:24.330
to 90-minute thing that I talked
about a few minutes ago, learn

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:28.050
to know when these occur and
really treat them as valuable,

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:31.230
maybe even wholly in the sense
that they are really the times

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:32.790
that are offered
up to you each day

00:22:32.790 --> 00:22:35.850
by your own biology in ways
that will allow you to get

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:37.800
better pretty much at anything.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.510
Thank you for joining
for the beginning

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:41.340
of this Ask Me Anything episode.

